  <h1>Repair</h1>
  <p class="lead">Paste messy JSON; Pandia fixes it.</p>

  <h2>What it fixes</h2>
  <p>Repair handles the common ways JSON arrives broken:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trailing commas</strong> after the last element or property.</li>
    <li><strong>Unquoted keys</strong> — bare identifiers used as object keys.</li>
    <li><strong>Single quotes</strong> around strings instead of double quotes.</li>
    <li><strong>Comments</strong> — both <code>//</code> line and <code>/* */</code> block.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-JSON literals</strong> — <code>NaN</code>, <code>Infinity</code> and
    <code>undefined</code> become <code>null</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Byte-order marks</strong> at the start of the file.</li>
    <li><strong>JSONP / parenthesis wrappers</strong> — strips the callback or paren wrapping.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing brackets and unterminated strings</strong> — closes what was left open. A
    key at the very end with no value is dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Line breaks and stray backslashes</strong> inside strings are escaped, so the text
    reads the same.</li>
    <li><strong>Cut-off JSON Lines</strong> — records on separate lines whose last one was cut off
    are joined into an array.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What it never changes</h2>
  <p>Repair changes only structure: brackets, commas, quotes and comments. The text inside a string
  is never touched, so a URL, an apostrophe or a <code>//</code> inside a value comes through as it
  is. The one exception is a broken character escape, such as half of an emoji
  (<code>\ud83d</code>), which no JSON reader can load: it becomes <code>�</code>, and the banner
  says so.</p>

  <h2>When it runs</h2>
  <p>When a file or paste isn't valid JSON, Pandia first reads it as JSONC or JSON5, then converts
  YAML, XML, CSV or a cURL command. If that fails and <strong>Settings → Behavior →
  auto-repair</strong> is on, it repairs the text, up to 200 MB. A banner above the document lists
  what changed in plain words.</p>
  <p>A repaired document opens as a copy named <code>name.repaired.json</code>, so Save asks where to
  keep it. The broken original is only replaced if you choose to save over it.</p>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Before — malformed input, with a comment, a single-quoted string, an unquoted key, and a
  trailing comma:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="p">&#123;</span>
  <span class="c">// user record</span>
  <span class="n">name</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="s">'Ada'</span><span class="p">,</span>
  <span class="n">"score"</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="k">NaN</span><span class="p">,</span>
<span class="p">&#125;</span></code></pre>
  <p>After — clean, valid JSON:</p>
  <pre><code><span class="p">&#123;</span>
  <span class="n">"name"</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="s">"Ada"</span><span class="p">,</span>
  <span class="n">"score"</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="k">null</span>
<span class="p">&#125;</span></code></pre>

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <p>Once the document is valid, read it in any of the <a href="/docs/views">five views</a> or
  check it against a schema with <a href="/docs/validate">Validate</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Related —</strong> the <a href="/json-repair">JSON repair</a> tool.</p>
